      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>A care home is only as safe as its least-trained shift. The matrix shows who is current on moving and handling, safeguarding, fire and more, and who is overdue. Its value is timing. It should show a lapse before it happens. The question is how to keep it true at inspection, not just in spring.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>A matrix tracks who is current on mandatory training and whose supervision is on time.</li><li>Inspectors look at whether staff are trained, supported and supervised. That is CQC well-led and staffing in England, and the same idea across the UK.</li><li>The real risk hides in bank and agency staff.</li><li>No single national law lists mandatory training for every role. Use your policies and current guidance.</li><li>The matrix trains nobody. Keeping it current and closing gaps does.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Currency",
      "heading": "Mandatory training, kept in date",
      "html": "<p>Every home has training staff must hold and refresh. Moving and handling. Safeguarding. Fire. Infection control. First aid. Medication where it applies. The list and gaps come from your policies and current guidance, not one national law.</p><p>A refresher that expires next month should show amber now, so it is booked before it lapses. A regular check surfaces what is due.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Supervision",
      "heading": "One-to-ones and appraisal on time",
      "html": "<p>Supervision is the other half. Inspectors ask about it. It is where competence is checked, worries raised and development planned. A certificate is not the same as skill on the floor.</p><p>Track when each person was last supervised and when it is next due. Include permanent, bank and agency staff. Supervisions slip quietly. A regular check catches it.</p>"
    },
